{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "moves.chaining",
    "description": "Template library: the arithmetic half of a CHAINED pollutant-process. MOVES computes some pollutants not from a rate table but from another pollutant already computed -- PM2.5 brake wear from PM10 by `PM10EmissionRatio`, NO and NO2 from total NOx by `NONO2Ratio`, crankcase from running by `CrankcaseEmissionRatio`, and the hydrocarbon species from THC by `methaneTHCRatio` and `HCSpeciation`. `runspecchainedto` is the edge list and `pollutantprocessassoc.chainedto1`/`chainedto2` the full graph it is pruned from.\n\nThe chain is a FIXED POINT and not a single hop: a chained pollutant-process can itself be a parent. The model unrolls it FOUR rungs, and four is not a corpus measurement but the closure of MOVES's own graph: the longest path in the complete on-road `pollutantprocessassoc` chainedTo graph, with the CO2AE and refueling edges its Java declares (conventions 77), outside SulfatePMCalculator's cycle and the particles chained off it, is FOUR (PLAN.md §7.3.72, measured over the default database's 786 rows: 107 pollutant-processes at depth 4, none deeper). Every depth-4 path is a PROCESS COPY on top of the three-rung species chain -- THC to NMHC to VOC to a toxic, then CrankcaseEmissionCalculator's copy of it at a crankcase process (`scale-county-idle-species`: benzene at crankcase extended idle) or a refueling species off its process's THC, itself chained from the energy at running, start and extended idle. The three species rungs are what the corpus measured before (`chain-tog-speciation`: THC, NMHC, NMOG, TOG; `process-airtoxics`: THC, NMHC, VOC, benzene); the widest fan-in is TWO parents (TOG is the SUM of methane and NMOG). `process-pm-exhaust` and every nonroad snapshot have chainedto graphs with CYCLES (118 from 115 and 115 from 118), because the table records which pollutants a calculator MAY derive an output from and not which it does; that cycle is SulfatePMCalculator's share matrix (conventions 76), not a fixed point, and docs/esm-conventions.md 71 says why a fixed-number unroll is the right reading of the rest.\n\nWHY UNROLLED AND NOT A RECURRENCE (conventions 124): a rung reads the PARENT'S row, another row of the rate relation, so a recurrence over a generation axis is a read at a non-identity index on a non-recurrence axis, which esm-spec §4.3.1.1 rejects (`recurrence_not_wellfounded`); a recurrence along the rate relation itself needs every parent row before its children, and the relation is in output order (benzene, 20, before VOC, 87). A closure operator would be a new AST operation. The fifth generation of the presence pass is the run's own check (`run_chainDepthExceeded`, asserted 0).\n\nA `faq`'s ranges and join clause are equation-level and no expression template can carry them, so the four passes themselves are spelled out at the call site. What IS shared is the per-pass PREDICATE, which is this file.",
    "authors": ["EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"],
    "tags": ["moves", "onroad", "chained-pollutants", "template-library"]
  },
  "expression_templates": {
    "chained_emits": {
      "params": ["survives", "has_chain_ratio", "parent_emits", "bin_is_selected"],
      "body": {
        "op": "max",
        "args": [
          "survives",
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "*", "args": ["has_chain_ratio",
              { "op": "ifelse", "args": [{ "op": ">", "args": ["parent_emits", 0.0] }, 1.0, 0.0] }] },
            "bin_is_selected"] }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
